.TH ZMRX 1 "August 30, 2026"
.SH NAME
zmrx \- receive file using ZMODEM protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B zmrx
[
.B \-7sebmMjnopdvq
] [
.B \-l\fIline\fP
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I zmrx
receives a file using the ZMODEM file transfer protocol.
By default,
.I zmrx
reads
.I stdin
and writes
.IR stdout.
Normal builds accept standard 1 KiB data subpackets and the 4 KiB and 8 KiB
subpackets commonly called ZedZap.  Builds compiled with
.B REDUCED_MEMORY
accept standard 1 KiB subpackets only; this does not limit file size.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-s
Request non-streaming operation.  The receiver clears its overlapped I/O
capability and advertises its configured maximum receive buffer (8 KiB
normally or 1 KiB with
.BR REDUCED_MEMORY ),
causing a conforming sender to wait for an acknowledgement after every data
subpacket.
.TP
.B \-e
Request that the sender escape all control characters.
.TP
.B \-b
Request Omen ZMODEM-90 seven-bit-safe transport.  The sender uses the
0x31 header format, RLE, CRC32, and SO/ZDLE eighth-bit quoting.  This is the
fallback format in the CP/M build.
.TP
.B \-7
Request Omen ZMODEM-90 Pack-7 and imply
.BR \-b .
Pack-7 encodes four bytes as five printable seven-bit characters and uses the
0x32 header format.  A sender which does not honor the request continues with
0x31 framing.  Pack-7 is the CP/M default.
.TP
.B \-m
Request Omen ZMODEM-90 MobyTurbo when the sender's active transparency probe
arrives unchanged.  The mode uses CRC32 and normally escapes only ZDLE.  The
sender must also explicitly offer MobyTurbo; use
.B \-M
to refuse all MobyTurbo offers.
.TP
.B \-M
Refuse Omen MobyTurbo.
.TP
.B \-l\fIline\fP
File (typically a tty) to use for the transfer instead
of
.IR stdin/stdout.
This option is supplied by the POSIX platform frontend; other platform
frontends may provide different transport options in the usage display.
.TP
.B \-j
Junk any path components of the filename: receive into the
base filename in the current directory.
.TP
.B \-n
Transfer only if source is newer than destination.
.TP
.B \-o,\-p
Overwrite (protect) the destination if it exists.
The default is to protect the destination.  These
flags interact in a complicated way with similar
options at the receiving end -- see the protocol
specification, or be careful.
.TP
.B \-v,\-q
Verbose (quiet) mode.  Quiet mode suppresses progress, debug, and transfer-error
diagnostics; exit status still reports failure.
.TP
.B \-d
Debug mode.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
The transfer completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Command-line usage was invalid.
.TP
.B 2
Local initialization or transfer-line setup failed.
.TP
.B 3
The initial protocol session could not be established.
.TP
.B 4
A file or protocol transfer failed.
.TP
.B 5
The transfer succeeded, but buffered output, terminal restoration, or transfer-line
close failed during cleanup.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PD
.BR  zmtx(1), sz(1), rz(1)
